The current position features an established pair of adjacent \(x\) pieces on row 4, columns 3-4, together with a single \(x\) on the bottom row at column 3. Vertical wins through columns 3 and 4 are already impossible (enemy pieces interrupt both columns and leave only three free cells above). Consequently the only fast routes to a three-in-a-row for \(x\) are:

- the horizontal extension of the row-4 pair, which requires a single preparatory stone on the bottom of column 2 (or 5) followed by a claim on row 4;
- the unique surviving bottom-row four-in-a-row window (columns 0-3), which is completed by stones at (5,2) and (5,1).

Playing column 2 realises both plans simultaneously. After \(x\) places at (5,2) the opponent has only one answer and therefore can stop at most one of the two plans:

- if the opponent answers in column 2 (claiming (4,2)), \(x\) continues with column 1, obtaining an immediate three on the bottom row whose open end (column 0) is already playable and therefore a forced threat;
- if the opponent answers in column 1 (or 0), \(x\) claims (4,2) and obtains the row-4 three; because column 1 already contains a stone the left-hand end of that three is playable, again producing a forced threat;
- any other reply lets \(x\) choose freely between the two forcing sequences above.

In every line \(x\) therefore obtains at least one live three-in-a-row while the opponent is still two moves away from his own first three (his pair sits on row 3 and needs three stones in a side column). The same dual-threat property does not exist for the symmetric right-hand column 5, nor for the less-central column 1; both of those moves allow the opponent a single answer that nullifies every immediate three for \(x\). Center columns 3 and 4 produce no three at all and leave the existing pair unextended. Edge columns are still slower.

Hence column 2 is the unique move that maximises the number of forced threats \(x\) can create on the subsequent turn and keeps the initiative.

Final Answer: 2

